**2. REAL DEFECT — Use-after-free / double-free on static spans, `src/widgets/span/lv_span.c:202-225` and `259-281`**

```c
if(span->txt == NULL && span->static_flag) {
    lv_free(span->txt);
}
```

The condition is backwards. Two real bugs here:

- **Freeing NULL is harmless** but the guard is inverted: it frees only when `span->txt == NULL`, which is the case where there's nothing to free. When a static text is actually set, `span->txt` points at the static (non-owned) string and `static_flag == 1`; the free is skipped, so the static string is leaked-into (not freed — correct) — but then:
- **Static text never gets replaced as owned:** the new `text` from `lv_text_set_text_vfmt` is assigned, `static_flag = 0`, `span->txt = text`. That part is fine. But the real defect is the condition should be `if(span->txt != NULL && span->static_flag)`. As written, when a span holds a truly-owned dynamic string with `static_flag == 0`, `span->txt = text` **overwrites the old owned pointer without freeing it** — a **memory leak** on every `lv_span_set_text_fmt` call on a previously-dynamic span. When it holds a static string (`static_flag == 1`, `txt != NULL`), it skips the free (correct not-to-free) and replaces — fine. So the net effect: dynamic→fmt leaks the old buffer.

Compare with the existing `lv_span_set_text` / `lv_span_set_text_static` in the same file: the correct pattern frees `span->txt` when it was owned, before replacing. This new code has the condition inverted. Concrete failure: calling `lv_span_set_text_fmt(span, "%d", n)` repeatedly in a loop leaks one buffer per iteration. **Kind: memory leak.** (The inverted `NULL &&` makes the free dead in the one case it's wanted and absent in the case it's needed.)

**9. REAL DEFECT — XML span bind: fmt duplicated + freed by a separate event callback that may not fire, `src/others/xml/parsers/lv_xml_spangroup_parser.c:91-104`**

```c
const char * fmt = lv_xml_get_value_of(attrs, "bind_text-fmt");
if(fmt) {
    fmt = lv_strdup(fmt);
    lv_obj_add_event_cb(spangroup, free_fmt_event_cb, LV_EVENT_DELETE, (void *) fmt);
}
lv_spangroup_bind_span_text(spangroup, span, subject, fmt);
```

This re-implements (locally, with a *new* `free_fmt_event_cb`) exactly the helper the rest of this very PR consolidated into `lv_event_free_user_data_cb` — the label parser was just refactored to use the shared helper (diff line `src/others/xml/parsers/lv_xml_label_parser.c:70-73`), yet the spangroup parser reintroduces a private copy. **Consistency/refactor item — the shared helper exists and should be reused.** Also: unlike the label path, the span path registers `free_fmt_event_cb` on the `spangroup` object rather than tying the free to the span's lifecycle — if the spangroup outlives the span, the strdup'd fmt is freed only when the group dies, but if the span is deleted first the bind still references... it's fine since fmt lives until group delete. Low severity.

**10. REAL DEFECT — `lv_xml_tabview_tab_button_create` crashes on missing/`NULL` index, `src/others/xml/parsers/lv_xml_tabview_parser.c:91-99`**

```c
const char * index_str = lv_xml_get_value_of(attrs, "index");
int32_t index_int = lv_xml_atoi(index_str);
```

If `index` attribute is absent, `index_str == NULL` and `lv_xml_atoi(NULL)` likely dereferences NULL. The XML schema (`lv_tabview.xml`) declares `index` as an arg but it's optional in XML. And the docs for `lv_tabview_get_tab_button` say "0 means start counting ... NULL if out of range" — but the parser's bounds check is `LV_ABS(index_int) >= btn_cnt` which mishandles negative indices: `lv_tabview_get_tab_button(tv, -1)` in `lv_tabview.c` uses `lv_obj_get_child_by_type(..., idx, ...)` where a negative idx from the back is then... but the parser has already rejected negatives with `LV_ABS` check → `LV_ABS(-1)=1`. If `btn_cnt==1`, `1 >= 1` → true → clamps to 0. OK. But the **NULL deref on missing index** is real. **Kind: crash (NULL deref) with concrete scenario: `<lv_tabview-tab_button/>` without an `index` attribute.**

**2. Real defect (memory leak) — reversed ownership-free condition in span text setters.**
`src/widgets/span/lv_span.c:202-225` (and the identical `259-281`)
```c
if(span->txt == NULL && span->static_flag) {
    lv_free(span->txt);
}
```
The guard is inverted: it frees only when `span->txt == NULL` (nothing to free) and skips the free precisely when the old text was an owned dynamic buffer (`txt != NULL`, `static_flag == 0`). Every `lv_span_set_text_fmt`/`lv_spangroup_set_span_text_fmt` call on a span that already holds a dynamic string overwrites `span->txt` in `span->txt = text` without freeing the previous buffer. Concrete scenario: a loop calling `lv_span_set_text_fmt(span, "%d", n)` leaks one buffer per iteration. The condition should be `txt != NULL && static_flag`.
